/* Unit 3: Personality and Psychological Disorders. Notes data and key terms for the revision site. */
window.UNIT={"id":"unit3","eyebrow":"Unit 3","title":"Personality and Psychological Disorders","sections":{"A":"Personality Theory","B":"Measuring Personality","C":"The Diathesis-Stress Model","D":"GAD and OCD","E":"Major Depressive Disorder","F":"Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder"},"terms":{"A":[{"term":"Personality","def":"A person's characteristic patterns of thinking, feeling, and behaving that stay relatively stable across time and situations."},{"term":"Id","def":"In Freud's model, the primitive, unconscious part of personality that seeks immediate gratification. It runs on the pleasure principle."},{"term":"Ego","def":"In Freud's model, the largely conscious part of personality that finds realistic ways to meet the id's demands. It runs on the reality principle."},{"term":"Superego","def":"In Freud's model, the internalized moral standards absorbed from parents and society. It produces guilt and shame."},{"term":"Defense Mechanisms","def":"Unconscious strategies the ego uses to manage anxiety by distorting or denying a threatening reality. The person is usually unaware of using them."},{"term":"Repression","def":"Blocking a threatening thought, memory, or desire from conscious awareness entirely."},{"term":"Projection","def":"Attributing your own unacceptable impulses or feelings to someone else."},{"term":"Rationalization","def":"Building a logical-sounding justification for a decision that was really driven by emotion."},{"term":"Displacement","def":"Redirecting an emotion from its original target to a safer substitute."},{"term":"Regression","def":"Going back to an earlier, less mature pattern of behavior under stress."},{"term":"Denial","def":"Refusing to acknowledge an uncomfortable reality, even while aware of it at some level."},{"term":"Self-Actualization","def":"The top of Maslow's hierarchy: fulfilling your potential and living in line with your deepest values."},{"term":"Self-Concept","def":"In Rogers's theory, your organized set of beliefs about who you are."},{"term":"Ideal Self","def":"In Rogers's theory, the person you feel you should be or wish to be."},{"term":"Congruence","def":"A match between your self-concept and your actual lived experience. Rogers linked it to psychological health."},{"term":"Incongruence","def":"A gap between your self-concept and your lived experience, often produced by rigid conditions of worth."},{"term":"Conditions of Worth","def":"The conditions a person believes they must meet to be accepted. When rigid, they lead people to hide their real experience."},{"term":"Unconditional Positive Regard","def":"Acceptance that does not depend on meeting any standard. Rogers saw it as necessary for genuine growth."},{"term":"Big Five (OCEAN)","def":"Five broad dimensions of personality found through decades of research: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism."},{"term":"Trait","def":"A relatively stable tendency that produces consistent patterns of thought, feeling, and behavior across situations."},{"term":"Reciprocal Determinism","def":"Bandura's idea that person factors, behavior, and environment all influence one another at the same time, so none is the single cause."},{"term":"Self-Efficacy","def":"Your belief in your ability to succeed at a specific task. It is domain-specific, unlike general self-esteem."},{"term":"Locus of Control","def":"How much a person believes outcomes are controlled by their own actions (internal) or by outside forces (external)."},{"term":"Explanatory Style","def":"The usual way a person explains the causes of good and bad events, ranging from optimistic to pessimistic."}],"B":[{"term":"Self-Report Inventory","def":"A structured questionnaire in which people rate their own traits, thoughts, or behaviors. Most Big Five tests use this method."},{"term":"Informant Report","def":"A personality rating completed by someone who knows the person well, such as a parent or close friend."},{"term":"Projective Test","def":"A method that asks people to respond to ambiguous images, such as inkblots, and treats the answers as clues to unconscious concerns."},{"term":"Behavioral Observation","def":"Watching what a person actually does in a natural or structured setting and coding it against set criteria."},{"term":"Semi-Structured Interview","def":"An interview that follows a flexible guide of open questions, with follow-up questions based on the answers given."},{"term":"Social Desirability Bias","def":"Answering questions about yourself in ways that make you look good, whether or not the answers are accurate."},{"term":"Confirmation Bias","def":"Noticing and giving more weight to evidence that supports what you already believe, while downplaying evidence against it."},{"term":"Interviewer Bias","def":"When an interviewer's expectations, tone, or body language shape the answers a participant gives."},{"term":"Participant Bias","def":"Distortion in an informant's report about someone else, shaped by their relationship, such as loyalty or wanting to avoid conflict."},{"term":"Observer Effect","def":"People behaving differently because they know they are being watched or studied."}],"C":[{"term":"Biopsychosocial Model","def":"The view that biological, psychological, and social factors interact to produce or protect against a psychological disorder."},{"term":"Diathesis-Stress Model","def":"The idea that a disorder develops when a pre-existing vulnerability is triggered by a stressor."},{"term":"Diathesis","def":"A long-standing biological or psychological vulnerability that exists before any triggering event and lowers the threshold for a disorder."},{"term":"Stressor","def":"A specific event or ongoing condition that triggers a disorder in a vulnerable person. The same event may have no clinical effect on someone else."}],"D":[{"term":"GAD","def":"Generalized Anxiety Disorder: excessive, hard-to-control worry across several areas of life, more days than not, for at least six months, with physical symptoms."},{"term":"OCD","def":"Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder: intrusive obsessions and compulsions done to reduce the anxiety, which take over an hour a day or cause significant distress or impairment."},{"term":"Obsession","def":"An unwanted, intrusive thought, image, or urge that keeps returning and causes anxiety."},{"term":"Compulsion","def":"A repeated behavior or mental act a person feels driven to perform to reduce the anxiety an obsession causes."},{"term":"Ego-Dystonic","def":"Recognizing your own thoughts as irrational or unwanted while being unable to stop them. It separates OCD from delusional thinking."},{"term":"Thought-Action Fusion","def":"The belief that having a thought is as bad as acting on it, or makes the event more likely. A thinking pattern linked to OCD."},{"term":"Intolerance of Uncertainty","def":"Finding not-knowing very hard to bear. A vulnerability factor shared by GAD and OCD."}],"E":[{"term":"MDD","def":"Major Depressive Disorder: five or more depressive symptoms over the same two weeks, including depressed mood or anhedonia, causing significant distress or impairment."},{"term":"Anhedonia","def":"Loss of interest in, or capacity to feel pleasure from, activities a person used to enjoy. A person can take part and still feel nothing."},{"term":"Beck's Negative Cognitive Triad","def":"Persistent negative views of the self, the world, and the future. Beck linked this thinking pattern to vulnerability to depression."},{"term":"Normal Bereavement","def":"Grief after a loss. On its own it does not meet the criteria for MDD, although loss can trigger MDD in a vulnerable person."}],"F":[{"term":"PTSD","def":"Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder: intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in thinking and mood, and heightened arousal after a traumatic event, lasting more than one month."},{"term":"Intrusion Symptoms","def":"Unwanted, distressing memories, nightmares, flashbacks, or strong body reactions to reminders of a trauma."},{"term":"Avoidance Symptoms","def":"Staying away from thoughts, feelings, people, places, or conversations linked to a trauma."},{"term":"Negative Cognition and Mood","def":"Distorted self-blame, persistent negative beliefs, loss of interest, and feeling cut off from others after a trauma."},{"term":"Arousal Symptoms","def":"Irritability, being constantly on guard, a strong startle response, poor sleep, and trouble concentrating after a trauma."},{"term":"Avoidant Coping","def":"Dealing with distress by pushing it away instead of working through it. Combined with low neuroticism, it can raise PTSD risk."}]},"related":[["Defense Mechanisms","Repression"],["Defense Mechanisms","Projection"],["Defense Mechanisms","Rationalization"],["Defense Mechanisms","Displacement"],["Defense Mechanisms","Regression"],["Defense Mechanisms","Denial"],["Defense Mechanisms","Ego"],["Incongruence","Conditions of Worth"],["Personality","Trait"],["Trait","Big Five (OCEAN)"],["Biopsychosocial Model","Diathesis-Stress Model"],["Diathesis-Stress Model","Diathesis"],["Diathesis-Stress Model","Stressor"],["OCD","Obsession"],["OCD","Compulsion"],["OCD","Ego-Dystonic"],["OCD","Thought-Action Fusion"],["GAD","Intolerance of Uncertainty"],["MDD","Anhedonia"],["MDD","Normal Bereavement"],["PTSD","Intrusion Symptoms"],["PTSD","Avoidance Symptoms"],["PTSD","Negative Cognition and Mood"],["PTSD","Arousal Symptoms"],["Avoidance Symptoms","Avoidant Coping"]],"mistakes":{"A":[{"think":"the Big Five explains where personality differences come from.","actually":"the Big Five is a taxonomy. It describes and predicts well, but explaining where traits come from needs the psychodynamic, humanistic, or social-cognitive frameworks."},{"think":"self-efficacy and self-esteem are the same thing.","actually":"self-efficacy is belief in succeeding at a specific task, while self-esteem is a general sense of your own worth. You can have high self-efficacy in writing and low self-efficacy in public speaking."},{"think":"reciprocal determinism means the environment is the real cause and the person just reacts.","actually":"Bandura rejects one-way causes. Person, behavior, and environment all shape one another at the same time."},{"think":"conditions of worth and unconditional positive regard are the same idea.","actually":"conditions of worth make acceptance depend on meeting a standard, which can produce incongruence. Unconditional positive regard is acceptance with no conditions at all."}],"B":[{"think":"participant bias and social desirability bias are the same thing.","actually":"social desirability bias is about describing yourself. Participant bias is about an informant describing someone else, shaped by their relationship with that person."},{"think":"confirmation bias only affects other people's research.","actually":"it applies to how you read your own results too, for example focusing only on memories that fit what you already believe about yourself."},{"think":"the observer effect is about questionnaires.","actually":"the observer effect is behavior changing because people know they are being watched, so it matters most in observation. For questionnaires, social desirability bias is the closer idea."},{"think":"if two methods only correlate moderately, one of them must be wrong.","actually":"a moderate correlation is often what you would expect. Both methods can pick up real information while each also carries its own separate biases."}],"C":[{"think":"the diathesis is whatever happened most recently in the scenario.","actually":"the diathesis is the long-standing vulnerability that was there before the trigger. The most recent, one-time event is usually the stressor."},{"think":"if a disorder involves worry, the diathesis must be high neuroticism.","actually":"only name a trait the scenario gives evidence for. A long-standing pattern of perfectionism or suppressed needs can be the diathesis without neuroticism being mentioned."},{"think":"symptoms such as lost sleep or withdrawal are part of the diathesis or the stressor.","actually":"symptoms are the outcome the model is trying to explain. They are neither the diathesis nor the stressor."}],"D":[{"think":"OCD is just a stronger version of liking things tidy.","actually":"OCD needs obsessions or compulsions that take over an hour a day or cause significant distress or impairment, and the person usually sees the thoughts as irrational. A preference for order with no impact on daily life is not OCD."},{"think":"strong worry about one upcoming event is GAD.","actually":"GAD is excessive, hard-to-control worry across several areas of life, more days than not, for at least six months."}],"E":[{"think":"MDD and grief are the same thing.","actually":"loss can trigger MDD in a vulnerable person, but normal bereavement on its own does not meet the criteria."},{"think":"anhedonia just means not wanting to do things.","actually":"a person with anhedonia can take part in an activity they used to enjoy and still feel nothing. It is the loss of pleasure, not simply a choice to skip."}],"F":[{"think":"anyone who goes through a trauma will develop PTSD.","actually":"PTSD develops in only some trauma survivors. The biopsychosocial model explains why the same event affects people so differently."},{"think":"high neuroticism is the only personality risk factor for PTSD.","actually":"low neuroticism combined with avoidant coping can also raise risk, and a lack of social support after the event is one of the strongest risk factors of all."}]}};
